## Answer to the criteria: **No — not production ready.** 29 defects open (17 critical), `crash_consistency` FAILs 0/32. Marker not written.

**What this session did.** sess127 handed over a measured root with the RULE-5 consult marked MANDATORY AND NOT YET DONE. I did the consult and started the build.

**New corroboration found before consulting** — `dlm_caw.h:117-125`, sess8's own comment:

> *"A flat 25ms cadence regressed cc@32 60->91s: **every hint fires bast_cb on all receivers (no dedup)**, and ~31 waiters x 40/s melted the cluster in callback processing."*

sess8 diagnosed this exact defect and tuned the send cadence down instead of fixing it. Two facts fall out: cc@32 has a real **60 s PASS baseline** (it now blows 90 s), and `MXFS_CAW_BAST_RESEND_FAST_MS`/`_FAST_COUNT` are **defined but never referenced** — dead constants.

**The ruling changed the design in one important way I had wrong.** I planned to fix only the UDP receive path. `bast_poll_fn` *also* calls `ctx->bast_cb` inline — so today the recv thread and the poll thread can run callbacks **for the same resource concurrently, with nothing serializing them**. Both producers must submit through the queue. Per-resource serialization is therefore something this change *adds*, not something it relaxes. Also ruled: 2 workers (exactly today's recv+poll concurrency, so the liveness class cannot widen); queue-based coalescing, no time window; conservative **lattice** join for the mode merge, because the modes are a partial order and `max(CW,PR)=PR` would silently drop a CW waiter's wakeup; defer the held-list prefilter (absence from non-authoritative memory is not proof of absence on disk) and the forensic offload.

**Landed** (`dlm/dlm_caw.{c,h}`, not yet built): the mode-join over the 6×6 `lock_compat` lattice; a preallocated pool + hash chains + FIFO ready queue with FREE/QUEUED/RUNNING and a tail-requeueing re-arm; `caw_bast_submit()` as the single producer entry point; the dispatcher; both producers converted; `P264-BASTQ-FULL`; and the poll relax gate now holds FAST after an overflow instead of relaxing straight back to 4000 ms.

One correction worth carrying forward: `POLL_RELAX_MS`'s recovery bound is not the interval. The loop reads a 256-slot window per pass, so a full rotation is `ceil(held.count/256) × interval` — at 4000 ms and a large held set that is minutes.

**Next session starts at lifecycle wiring**, which is the only reason none of this runs yet: `caw_bastq_init` in create, `caw_bastq_free` in both unwind paths, dispatcher creation in `start` *before* the poll thread, and joins in stop PHASE 3 *after* both producers. Then rev to 0.11.453, build, deploy, measure against the unchanged PASS bar. Ledger and design captured in `ccloop-c7ee71c6-sess128-GPT-ruling-bast-dispatch-queue-design`.

